beauty expert and youtube star nick  chapman was diagnosed with ms a few  years ago last month she took the  decision to fly to mexico for a new  treatment that isn't available on the  nhs at nick is with us now hello  hello  tell me more tell me about the treatment  so um there's a treatment for ms called  hsct it's the same treatment that selma  blair had recently  and i've had ms for  10 plus years but in the last couple of  years  i noticed it was getting a lot worse  ms is multiple sclerosis and it's a  disease and illness which stops the  neurological pathways from going from  your brain to different parts of your  body so it stops the signal going there  so it's very debilitating and everyone  has ms in a different kind of way  um i looked into having hsct done in the  uk  and it's impossible unless you go  privately and it is just so ridiculously  expensive  in this country to have it done so i  took the option to fly to mexico to have  the treatment done because mexico and  russia are the leaders in this treatment  and what does the treatment involve  um you have  about four or five rounds of  chemotherapy to  kill off the active ms that's in your  body and then they  take your stem cells give you more chemo  to kill off what's left there that isn't  working properly and reboot your immune  system and then they give you your stem  cells back hoping that no longer your  body will remember that it was fighting  itself  so  is this like a cure for ms then  they can't say cure but it has an 80  success rate and it's in its infancy so  although  mexico have been doing it for 10 15  years  they don't know the full outcome but for  me it has an 80 chance of working and  it's  saves me having to take drugs for the  rest of my life if this works then my ms  will be halted i will stay where i am  now which is still mobile and still  slight  cognitively  able  not always but um it will mean that my  ms won't get any worse and i just really  want to try and raise awareness for this  to try and also get neurologists in this  country to know a little bit more about  it so that they when people who are  living with ms like me go in to see  their neurologist they don't feel like  they're just getting completely purposed  about something that is such a brilliant  um hopefully life-changing treatment  yeah because when you think about ms you  think oh you know the brits or the  americans are going to be world leaders  on treatments not the russians and the  mexicans  yeah it's very very true um and  we do have the treatment here and  actually the drugs that are given to us  in the uk which is amazing i don't want  to um be against drugs because they work  so brilliantly for so many people  um but there is another thing that is  groundbreaking and it's actually much  cheaper for the nhs than the drugs that  people are living on so the drugs that  are offered to you  are brilliant and work for some people  but they can cost sixty thousand pounds  a year whereas people living with ms  could have this treatment done and it  only costs the nhs forty thousand pounds  for the treatment and they may never  have to take drugs again  so why do you think we don't do that  i don't i don't i honestly don't know  i'm trying to look into it because you  can do have it done privately  but there's a lot of people that can't  afford that because in this country it  costs about 100 000 pounds to have it  done privately  um  i think the knowledge probably isn't  there it's already done for  leukemia  um i also think that probably ms is a  it's slightly different for leukemia but  ms is a long debilitating disease  whereas a lot of the people that do get  stem cells their outcome is quite quick  and fast  so i think they just think we'll just  see whether we can manage it with drugs  for a long time but for someone living  with ms  they don't always have a long time and  that that downfall is just  horrific to  notice changes in your body every day  and  brain that doesn't work all the time and  not being able to play with your  children it's um  it's a long slow  illness which is just heartbreaking so i  wish that they would and that's why i'm  trying to talk out as much as i can  about hsct to try and  get neurologists to know a little bit  more about it and not be so scared of it  and to get people living with ms and  people in general to understand ms and  know about this treatment  so what happens next then nick  um  i am  as you can see i am only a couple of  months back i'm now starting to get a  little bit of energy back and um  feeling really good but it's going to be  a long roller coaster i have to have a  couple of mris to see whether my my  lesions are no longer active  um  i feel really good in my body whereas  when i first came back i didn't so much  um  i feel really good and it's brilliant  because there are some people that have  had this treatment like i've reached out  with selma blair and we've spoken a few  times about the treatment and she said  just hang on in there it takes a long  time for it to start to work and there's  an 80 success rate for me so i have  really high hopes for it and that i will  never have to go on  drugs again and i will be able to live  hopefully a drug-free happy healthy life  amazing i mean just before i let you go  how challenging was it going to mexico  because of covid and the fact that of  course that it was on the red route  it was so challenging well when we got  there mexico wasn't on the red list and  as i started my second round of chemo  mexico got put on the red list  and i'm sitting there having chemo and  was like  my husband was like mexico on the red  list i was like well we're too far in  now i don't know what to do so um  it took about a month and finally the  government got back to us to say i'd be  exempt because obviously when you come  back i had no immunity at all and i was  neutropenic so i had i couldn't stay in  a hotel because i had to be so careful  so we were lucky that we managed to make  it home but actually while i was there i  felt incredibly safe because i was in my  own little bubble with no one around  coming home has been more of a challenge  okay look after yourself great to talk  to you keep in touch and let us know how  you get on thank you take care
 

